.TH ODIN-SEAT-PROTOCOLS 7 2026-10-15 "Odin Seat 0.1.0" "Miscellaneous"
.SH NAME
odin-seat-protocols \- what a program running in a odin-seat window can use
.SH DESCRIPTION
Every
.BR odin-seat (1)
window is a terminal of its own, with the kitty protocol extensions
implemented per window.
Whatever is better done by the real terminal you attach from is handed
to it, and its answer is routed back to the window that asked.
A program never sees the multiplexer's own traffic and never talks to
the host directly.
This page lists what a program may rely on.
Sequences are written with
.B CSI
for ESC [,
.B OSC
for ESC ],
.B DCS
for ESC P,
.B APC
for ESC _ and
.B ST
for ESC \e.
.SH IDENTITY
.TP
.B TERM
.BR xterm\-256color ,
or
.BR $ODIN_SEAT_TERM .
.B COLORTERM
is
.BR truecolor .
.TP
XTVERSION
.B CSI > q
is answered
.BR "DCS > | OdinSeat " version " ST" .
.TP
DA1
.B CSI c
reports a VT level with sixel when any attached terminal can show images.
.TP
XTGETTCAP
.B DCS + q
.I hexname
[;
.IR hexname ...]
.B ST
is answered one
.B DCS 1 + r
per known name and
.B DCS 0 + r
per unknown one.
Known:
.BR TN " (the TERM), " Tc ", " RGB ", " Su " (true), " colors " (256), " setrgbf ", " setrgbb ", " Smulx ", " Setulc ", " Ms .
.TP
XTSMGRAPHICS
The graphics geometry reported is the size at which images will
actually be shown: the host's pixels for a kitty host, the window's
cell grid for a sixel host.
.SH KEYBOARD
Keys arrive as a legacy terminal would send them until the program
pushes kitty keyboard flags.
.B CSI > flags u
pushes,
.B CSI < n u
pops
.I n
(default 1),
.B CSI = flags ; mode u
sets (mode 1 replaces, 2 ors in, 3 clears) and
.B CSI ? u
is answered
.BR "CSI ? flags u" .
The stack holds eight entries and evicts the oldest; a pop past the
bottom resets every flag.
The main and alternate screens have separate stacks.
All five flags are honoured: disambiguated escapes, key release and
repeat events, alternate keys, all keys as escapes (modifier keys
included) and associated text.
Enter, Tab and Backspace stay legacy unless all keys are escapes; the
arrow, Home/End, F1\(enF12, Insert/Delete and Page keys keep their
legacy CSI forms with modifiers and event types appended.
.PP
The host terminal is run with every enhancement on, so this works
whenever the host speaks the protocol (Ghostty, kitty).
On another terminal keys are legacy whatever the program asked.
.SH MOUSE
Modes 1000, 1002 and 1003 track clicks, drags and any motion; 1006
reports SGR cell coordinates and 1016 SGR pixel coordinates. The pixel
is the one the host reported, including its position inside the cell,
translated into the window. A host that only reports cells yields the
centre of the cell.
Coordinates are the window's own.
A window in pixel mode is sent kitty's leave event,
.BR "CSI < 288 ; x ; y M" ,
when the pointer moves off it or leaves the terminal.
Mode 1007 (alternate scroll, on by default) turns the wheel into arrow
keys on the alternate screen; a window with no tracking scrolls its own
scrollback instead.
Mode 2004 wraps pastes and drops in
.B CSI 200 ~
and
.BR "CSI 201 ~" .
Focus reporting (1004) follows the active visible window and host terminal
focus. Switching windows or detaching sends focus-out; focus-in requires an
attached focused host. Reports propagate through nested sessions.
.SH TEXT
Styled and coloured underlines:
.BR "SGR 4:1" \(en "4:5" ,
.B SGR 58
(colour) and
.BR "SGR 59" .
.B SGR 221
and
.B 222
reset bold and faint independently.
DECCARA
.RB ( "CSI Pt ; Pl ; Pb ; Pr ; attrs $ r" )
changes attributes over a rectangle or a stream, DECSACE
.RB ( "CSI Ps * x" )
chooses which.
.B CSI 22 J
clears the screen into the scrollback and
.B CSI n + T
unscrolls
.I n
lines back out of it.
.B CSI ? Pm s
and
.B CSI ? Pm r
save and restore private modes (all side-effect-free ones when no
parameter is given).
OSC 66 text sizing lays a run into the grid at its scaled size and is
re-emitted to a host that speaks it, with its fractional scale
.RB ( n ", " d )
and alignment
.RB ( v ", " h ).
A window keeps every run its screen and scrollback still show.
.PP
Synchronized output (mode 2026) makes what a program draws between
.B CSI ? 2026 h
and
.B CSI ? 2026 l
one frame: the window is not drawn while the update is under way, and is
drawn the moment it ends.
An update that is not ended holds the window for 150 ms at most.
.B CSI ? Pm $ p
(DECRQM) reports a private mode kept here as set (1) or reset (2), and
any other as not recognised (0).
A program may resize its window with
.BR "CSI 8 ; rows ; cols t" ,
capped to the desktop and shifted on-screen;
.BR "CSI 14 t" ", " "CSI 16 t" " and " "CSI 18 t"
report pixel, cell and grid sizes.
.SH COLOURS
OSC 4, 10, 11 and 12 set (and with
.B ?
query) palette entries and the default foreground, background and cursor
colours; 104, 110, 111 and 112 reset them.
OSC 21 is kitty's colour control with the same keys.
OSC 30001 pushes the whole colour state and OSC 30101 pops it; the stack
has no maximum depth.
Silkie's deep colours,
.BR "38:0:" id : r : g : b
(and 48 and 58), give sixteen bits a channel; a cell keeps them exactly,
and a terminal that lists
.B deep
in its
.B sgr
capability field is sent them after the eight-bit colour, in a sequence of
their own, while any other is sent the nearest eight-bit colour.
All of this is per window: the compositor remaps that window's cells, so
two windows can redefine the palette differently and the host's own
palette is never touched.
The cursor colour is applied to the host for the focused window.
.SH PROGRAM STATUS
OSC 7501, the Program Status Protocol (revision 0.4), is kept per window:
one record per id, each replaced whole by its next report, applying the
protocol's lifetime rules (working, blocked and idle end at the next
prompt; done and error stay until the window is focused or typed in; RIS
removes all) and none of its size limits.
The feature query
.B "OSC 7501 ; ?"
is answered here.
A framed window shows its most urgent record after its title \(em the
percentage or an ellipsis while working, a check mark when done, a cross on
error, black on yellow when blocked on the user \(em with the number of
records in that state when more than one, and what the record says along
its bottom border.
A terminal that answers the feature query when a client attaches is sent
every window's records beneath an id of the window's own,
.BR w N / id ,
and as its root record the most urgent of them all, titled by its window;
they are cleared there when a window closes and when the client detaches.
OSC 9;4 progress is a window's root record until its program sends a
report of its own.
.SH CLIPBOARD AND NOTIFICATIONS
OSC 52 (set and read) and kitty's OSC 5522 go to the terminal, wrapped
for an outer tmux when nested; a read's answer comes back to the window
that asked.
Desktop notifications (OSC 99) go to the terminal. OSC 9 and OSC 777
notify;title;body are converted to OSC 99 with focus and report actions.
An OSC 9 that starts with a number from 1 to 12 and then
.B ;
or its end is a ConEmu sub-command:
.B 2
is a notification of its text,
.B 3
sets the window title (empty restores it),
.B 4
is progress, kept as the window's program status rather than notified,
.B 9
records the working directory (as OSC 7 does, which new split windows
start in),
.B 12
marks a prompt like OSC 133;A, and the others are ignored.
A click restores the originating window, including hidden or aside windows;
converted notices consume the reply rather than sending unexpected input
into the application.
.PP
Each hop appends
.B odin-seat/session-token/window-id
in OSC 99 metadata
.BR r .
Slash separates fields and comma separates hops, from the application outward.
Silkie appends its own native-window hop on activation and returns the full
route plus the effective requested actions in
.BR a .
Focus is restored from outside inward only when requested; report-only
notifications do not steal focus. Rich OSC 99 callers receive their original
ID, selected button, and complete route when they request report.
.PP
Notification IDs are wrapped in opaque self-routing addresses, one per layer,
so routing does not evict pending notices or truncate IDs. The session token
survives live server upgrades. Replies to closed windows or stale sessions
are discarded rather than guessed onto the focused window. Older hosts can
restore converted notices by their wrapped ID; full path and action reporting
requires Silkie's notification routing extension.
.PP
Codex can use this path with these settings in ~/.codex/config.toml:
.nf
[tui]
notifications = true
notification_method = "osc9"
notification_condition = "unfocused"
.fi
Start a new Codex session after changing its configuration.
A read with nobody attached is answered empty at once.
.SH POINTER SHAPES
OSC 22 sets, pushes
.RB ( > ),
pops
.RB ( < )
and queries
.RB ( ? )
the pointer shape as a stack per window.
The host shows the shape of the window under the pointer, or of the
focused window over the backdrop.
.SH EXTRA CURSORS
kitty's multiple cursors protocol:
.B CSI > shape ; type : coords ; ... SP q
with shapes 0 (clear), 1 block, 2 beam, 3 underline and 29 (as the main
cursor), coordinates as
.I y:x
points (type 2), top:left:bottom:right rectangles (type 4) or the main
cursor's cell (type 0); 30 and 40 set the text and cursor colours; 100
and 101 query positions and colours;
.B CSI > SP q
lists what is supported.
Cursors clear on ED 2, 3 and 22, on RIS and on switching screens.
A host that draws extra cursors is sent the visible set translated to
the desktop; one that cannot shows them as reverse video or underline.
.SH GRAPHICS
Each window is a kitty graphics endpoint: it stores what the program
transmits (any medium: direct, file, temporary file, shared memory;
PNG or raw pixels; chunked), answers its queries, and keeps placements
floating over the grid, including Unicode placeholders.
Sixel (DCS q) is decoded into the same placements.
What the terminal is shown depends on what it speaks: kitty graphics
re-emitted under a host-global id, or the placement rasterised to sixel
at the window's cell grid, or nothing.
A placement moves with its window, is clipped by windows above it, scrolls
with the text, and goes with ED 2, the alternate screen or a delete.
.PP
A window whose placements go under its text (a negative
.BR z ),
start part way into a cell
.RB ( X ", " Y )
or overlap one another is
.IR layered :
more than placeholder cells can say.
A kitty graphics terminal whose cells are the size the programs were told
is sent each of its images once and each placement as a real one, at its
cell, offset, source rectangle and
.BR z ,
cut to the cells where the window shows: in several pieces under another
window's frame or a menu, each with its part of the source, and none at
all for a window wholly covered, hidden or set aside.
A placement made again under its id moves without its pixels being sent
again.
A placement fitted to cells
.RB ( c ", " r )
is sent as the program made it while it shows whole; cut, its pieces are
whole cells, each with its share of the source.
With several terminals attached, programs draw for the largest cell among
them.
A kitty graphics terminal with another cell is sent each picture scaled to
its own, and the placements with their offsets and source rectangles in its
own pixels.
A vector scene
.RB ( f=200 )
is sent as the same records at the scaled size, its root group
.RB ( "g id=0" )
carrying the program's transform followed by the scaling; an edit of the
root group reaches such a terminal as the whole scene sent again.
Only a GPU surface goes as it is, fitted to its cells for the terminal to
scale.
A terminal that shows sixel has no placements to layer: the window's
pictures are blended into one canvas of its pixels, at their offsets and
.BR z ,
and the sixels are cut from that, a cell with text keeping its text.
Only the rectangles whose pixels changed are sent again.
Pixels in a wider gamut
.RB ( G )
are accepted when any attached terminal takes them, and sent as the
nearest sRGB to one that does not; a colour in a wider gamut
.RB ( "38:2:1" )
likewise.
The display programs are told of is the best of every terminal's: its
colour model, gamut, headroom and channel widths (and the
.B output_bits
of the surface it draws into) are each the most any of them has.
The capability reply lists
.B sgr=alpha,deep,gamut,hdr
whatever is attached: those attributes are kept in every cell and brought
down for a terminal that lacks them.
A vector scene
.RB ( f=200 )
or HDR pixels
.RB ( f=64 )
are accepted whatever is attached: a terminal that takes neither is sent
the scene drawn as pixels, at the size it shows it and again when it is
edited, and HDR as what eight bits show.
A text record of a scene
.RB ( t )
is laid out here, shaped by HarfBuzz in the fonts of the machine the server
runs on, a family it lacks falling back to the font of its role.
With no terminal attached that draws scenes, the vector service
.RB ( a=v )
is answered here from the same layout:
.BR fonts ", " inspect ", " upload ", " release " and " measure .
A raster image inside a scene
.RB ( i )
is not drawn.
A terminal with neither kitty graphics nor sixel is drawn every picture as
coloured half-block cells, unless
.B cell-pictures
is off.
Images are offered to programs when some attached terminal can show them
in one of these ways; DA1 and the kitty query say so.
.SH SILKIE
Silkie's OSC 777 extensions
.RB ( caps ", " depth ", " display ", " font ", " frame ", " gesture ", " haptic ", " ink ", " latency ", " scroll " and " tablet-pointer )
are answered by
.B odin-seat
itself.
A program is offered a selector only when this program can carry it and
some attached terminal listed it.
Frames have no length limit of their own.
Reports are translated into the window the contact began on, including
its frame and scroll, and a contact is cancelled when that window
moves, resizes, closes or is reset.
Modes belong to the window; the terminal is sent the union.
Picture formats and transmission media are whatever every attached
terminal shares, so a picture one of them cannot show is refused.
The formats that are decoded here are the exception: they are always
taken and always offered.
They are kitty's indexed colour
.RB ( f=8 ", " f=16 ),
drawn in the window's own colours, Silkie's packed pixels
.RB ( f=301 ", " f=302 ", " f=304 ", " f=308
gray and
.B f=516
RGB565), its palette images
.RB ( f=401 ", " f=402 ", " f=404 ", " f=408 ,
whose palette
.B a=e
replaces), and its high precision pixels
.RB ( f=316 ", " f=632 ", " f=664 )
with the transfer function key
.BR E .
The numbers Silkie used before 2026-11-16
.RB ( f=1 ", " f=2 ", " f=4 )
are refused.
Images have no size or storage limit but the arithmetic and memory;
.B S
and
.B O
are 64-bit.
A feature query
.RB ( a=q
with
.BR e= )
is answered with the keys taken for that action.
.PP
Each kitty graphics terminal is sent each picture packed for its own
display, as its display reply describes it: on a gray or black and white
display (or one painted with
.BR \-m )
as gray at the levels it shows, on a display with fewer than eight bits a
channel (RGB565, say) at those widths.
Nothing the display cannot show is sent.
Then the narrowest format that holds what is left exactly, among those the
terminal listed: one, two, four or eight bits of gray, RGB565, a palette
image of its colours, RGB without
alpha when every pixel is opaque, RGBA otherwise; deflated
.RB ( o=z )
when that is smaller.
The program's own form is sent instead when nothing was reduced and it is
no bigger.
Indexed colour goes as it is to a terminal that lists it, in that
terminal's colours, unless its window set colours of its own; it is sent
again when they change.
Pixels of more than eight bits, or with another curve, go as they are to a
terminal that lists them, as half floats
.RB ( f=64 )
to one that takes HDR pixels, and as eight bits of sRGB to the rest.
Each form is worked out once per picture and display.
A terminal is asked about its display when it attaches and again whenever
its client reports a resize, and a display event from it counts the same;
when the answer changes, its pictures are sent again.
Its channel widths
.RB ( red_bits ", " green_bits ", " blue_bits ", " gray_bits ", " precision )
reach programs that ask.
OSC 133
.B A
and a caps reset clear that window's modes; OSC 133
.B A
also returns its stereo depth to the terminal plane.
The earlier
.B goblin-pointer
and
.B goblin-download
uses of OSC 777 are unchanged.
.PP
.B font
selection, and its SGR 10 to 19 aliases, is a text attribute.
Role, weight and slant stay with each cell through scrollback, resizing
and a live upgrade, and are painted to the terminals that listed
.BR font.1 ;
another terminal attached beside them sees ordinary text.
The query answers with the roles of every attached terminal and the
ranges all of them accept.
.PP
.B depth
is Silkie's stereo depth: a text attribute giving binocular disparity in
millidegrees
.RB ( d= )
or reference pixels
.RB ( p= ),
and vertical phase
.RB ( y= ).
An update is validated as a whole; an omitted attribute keeps its value.
Both stay with each cell through scrollback, resizing, the saved cursor
and a live upgrade, and are reset by SGR 0,
.BR op=reset ,
RIS and OSC 133
.BR A .
They are painted to the terminals that listed
.BR depth.1 ;
another terminal sees the text flat.
A kitty image placement takes the depth in effect when it is made, and is
made at that depth at the terminal; a vector image's
.BR depth= ,
.B depth_px=
and
.B phase=
keys travel inside its records.
Painting order and text layout are unchanged.
.PP
A vector image
.RB ( f=200 )
is kept as its table of records, with what is inside the records (shaped
text, box clips, HDR and wide gamut paints) carried unread, apart from the
fonts and images they name.
An edit
.RB ( a=e )
goes to the terminals that hold the scene, without sending it again; one
that attaches later is sent the scene as it stands.
The vector service
.RB ( a=v )
is answered by the first attached terminal that takes vector images and
routed back to the window that asked.
Session fonts belong to the window that uploaded them, under names of
this program's choosing at the terminal, and the name a program gave is
what it reads back.
A released font is kept until no scene uses it, since a terminal attaching
later has to shape those scenes itself; layouts that name it fail at once.
Strokes a terminal draws for itself with
.B ink
are worked out from its reports and added to the table, so other terminals
see them as they are drawn and a terminal sent the scene again has them.
A stroke goes when it is cancelled, and moving or resizing a window cancels
the strokes in it.
A terminal inks an image only through the cells it was sent, so ink stays
inside the window and under any window above it; a stroke that leaves the
window goes on, and what falls outside is not shown.
Each terminal holds the one brush its pen is about to need: for the placement
under the pen if that window has a brush, and otherwise for the focused
window's placement of its brush's image, the
.B U=1
one or else the direct one on top.
A pen that comes down on another without hovering first is not inked, and the
program draws that stroke itself.
A terminal is given ids of Odin Seat's for its strokes, each standing for one
window's brush on one placement, so programs may choose the same ids and any
number of terminals may ink at once.
The program's own id goes in the report, the next of those it set aside, and
when the stroke ends the terminal's element becomes the table's record under
that id; if that could not leave the same drawing order, as when the program
drew during the stroke, that terminal is sent the scene again.
A program that turns ink off during a stroke stops the terminal drawing it, and
a change of focus does not cut a stroke short.
While a stroke is inked its reports give the program the terminal's numbers by
the protocol's formulas, even where a whole number of cells or another shape
of cell shows the placement somewhat differently from what the program asked
for, so a position in such a report can differ from the pen's by part of a
cell.
Ink a window's program would not hear of, as for a contact held back as
accidental, is deleted at once.
Images, scenes and session fonts do not survive
.BR upgrade .
.PP
An OSC 22 image shape names an image of the window, which goes to the
terminal under an id of ours.
While the window has no such image the shape below it on the stack shows.
The
.B kitty-query-hdr_headroom
XTGETTCAP query is answered with the headroom the display last reported.
.SH FILE TRANSFER
kitty's file transfer protocol (OSC 5113) works in both directions, and
goblin-mosh's background download (OSC 777
.BR goblin-download )
towards the user.
When the terminal you attach from speaks the protocol \(en kitty for
5113, goblin-mosh for 777, known by their XTVERSION names \(en the
request is handed to it and its replies routed back.
Otherwise
.B odin-seat
is the endpoint: a box asks whether to save the files into the downloads
directory
.RB ( $ODIN_SEAT_DOWNLOAD_DIR ", " $XDG_DOWNLOAD_DIR ", or " ~/Downloads )
or, for a receive, whether the program may read the files it named.
Files are staged, inflated when sent compressed, size-checked,
published without overwriting and never opened or run.
A pasted
.B y
cannot approve.
.PP
Statuses follow the protocols:
.BR OK ", " STARTED ", " PROGRESS ", " CANCELED
and
.BI E CODE :message
for 5113; for 777 a query is answered
.B status=supported
with
.BR max_chunk=4096 ", " max_size=67108864 " and " approval=1 ,
a confirmed begin gets
.B approved
once permitted, a complete stream
.B queued
then
.B saved
with the final name, and refusal or failure
.B error
with a message.
rsync deltas are declined with
.BR EINVAL .
.SH DRAG AND DROP
kitty's drag and drop protocol (OSC 72).
The terminal is told what is accepted; each drag is routed to the window
under it and translated to that window's cells and pixels.
A program that announced
.B t=a
gets the motion, drop, data and completion messages and its answers are
relayed.
Otherwise the drop is taken on the window's behalf: files as their
paths, quoted for a shell when needed, or plain text, pasted into that
window, bracketed if the program asked for bracketed paste.
A terminal that delivers drops as a bracketed paste still has the paste
routed to the focused window.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR odin-seat (1)
.PP
The kitty terminal protocol extensions:
.I https://sw.kovidgoyal.net/kitty/protocol-extensions/

.SH SSH AGENT TRANSPORT
OSC 777 selector ssh-agent, version 1, carries complete SSH agent packets in
base64, including their four-byte big-endian length. query/cap discovers the
provider's availability and generation; request/reply carries a packet with
request id, connection token, generation, and an absolute Unix-millisecond
expiry. close retires a connection without a reply. The caps listing includes
ssh-agent.1 independently of provider availability.
.PP
New windows receive a stable per-session SSH_AUTH_SOCK. Agent requests go only
to the most recently attached display client, independently of pane focus.
On disconnect, the previous attached display is selected. A provider change
fails pending requests and existing connections; reopening the same socket
gets the new client's keys. IDs and connections are scoped across nested
windows and replies are never injected as keyboard input. Socket path,
listener, selection order and routing scope survive live server upgrades;
connections can be reopened after upgrade.
.PP
The global -A option authorizes the client's local SSH_AUTH_SOCK as a fallback
when the terminal has no provider, including agents forwarded by ordinary SSH.
Enable ssh_agent_forwarding yes in Silkie. Silkie uses the local SSH_AUTH_SOCK
or starts an owned ssh-agent and exports its socket to new local shells.
Requests may list keys, sign, or use session-bind@openssh.com and query
extensions. Backend connections persist so OpenSSH bindings are retained;
key mutation, smartcard loading and locking are refused. Agent access grants
terminal-output writers signing privileges, like ordinary agent forwarding.
.PP
Private keys remain at the provider. No key lists or signatures are cached.
Stale, closed, duplicate and expired requests fail without retrying a signing
action. Socket directories are mode 0700 and sockets mode 0600. Storage grows
with input and active connections. See docs/SSH_AGENT_PROTOCOL.md and
https://silkie.goblinreactor.com/ssh-agent-protocol.html for the wire format.
